 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Depends on the number of fragments per pixel and the complexity of volume rendering. Early termination of volume rendering can significantly reduce computation time for fully occluded pixels [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Proportional to the number of fragments stored in the A-buffer. High fragment counts can lead to significant memory usage [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Efficiently handles hybrid scenes with both surfaces and volumes. Early termination optimizes volume rendering. Physically-based lighting and shadows enhance realism [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** A-buffer management can be complex and memory-intensive. Performance may degrade with high fragment density [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to Use:** Suitable for visualizing procedural gases or fluids alongside explicit polygonal geometry where correct depth ordering is critical [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
+*   **Performance:** Described as a "fast" algorithm for surface-defined objects [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Efficiently combines surface and volume rendering; uses 3D shadow tables for efficient volumetric shadowing [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Not given in the sources.
+*   **When to use:** When visualizing procedural gases or fluids alongside surface-defined objects in a hybrid scene [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The method was developed by David Ebert as part of a system for procedural modeling, animation, and rendering of gases, fluids, and textures [S1, unofficial]. It builds upon earlier work in solid texturing and volume rendering [S1, unofficial]. The use of 3D shadow tables for volumetric shadows was introduced in earlier work by Ebert et al. [S1, unofficial].
+The method was developed by David Ebert as part of a system for procedural modeling, animation, and rendering of gases, fluids, and textures [S1, unofficial]. It builds upon work in solid texturing and procedural noise functions (such as Perlin’s turbulence) [S1, unofficial]. The approach was presented in the context of visualizing computational fluid dynamics and hybrid scenes [S2, unofficial].
 
